      sections: [{"id":"s1","title":"Grid Reference System","whyItMatters":"Every formula and function depends on accurate cell references. Understanding the A1 notation and range selection is foundational to all spreadsheet work.","content":"**Core Concepts:**\n\nGoogle Sheets uses the A1 reference system. Columns are labeled A-Z, then AA-AZ, BA-BZ, etc. Rows are numbered 1, 2, 3... (up to 10 million). A cell is identified by its column letter + row number, e.g., B3 means column B, row 3.\n\n**Range Notation:**\n- Single cell: A1\n- Column range: A:A (all of column A)\n- Row range: 1:1 (all of row 1)\n- Rectangular range: A1:C10\n- Multiple ranges: A1:A10, C1:C10\n\n**Selection Shortcuts:**\n\n```text\nShortcut           Action\nCtrl+Space         Select entire column\nShift+Space        Select entire row\nCtrl+A             Select all\nShift+Click        Extend selection\nCtrl+Shift+Arrow   Jump to data edge\n```\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n- Column headers use letters; rows use numbers\n- Use colons for ranges (A1:B10)\n- Double-click column/row borders to auto-fit content size","codeExamples":[{"id":"ex1","title":"Reference Practice","description":"Practice cell references","code":{"google-sheets":"// In cell A1, type: Product\n// In cell A2, type: Widget A\n// In cell B2, type: 100\n// In cell C2, type: =B2*2\n// Select range A1:C10 using the reference box"},"explanation":"Cell references like A1 and ranges like A1:C10 are the foundation of all formulas."}]}],
