      sections: [{"id":"s1","title":"Data Entry Fundamentals","whyItMatters":"Entering data correctly by type ensures formulas calculate properly and data stays clean. Autofill saves massive time when creating sequential data.","content":"**Core Concepts:**\n\nSheets automatically detects data types. Text is left-aligned by default; numbers and dates are right-aligned.\n\n**Data Types:**\n- Text: Any string (e.g., \"Product A\", \"New York\")\n- Numbers: Integers and decimals (e.g., 42, 3.14)\n- Dates: Type 1/1/2024 or =DATE(2024,1,1)\n- Times: Type 9:30 AM or 14:00\n- Booleans: TRUE or FALSE (automatically uppercase)\n\n**Autofill:**\n\n```text\nType in A1: Monday\nClick and drag the blue square at bottom-right\nSheets auto-fills: Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday...\n\nWorks with:\n- Days of week\n- Months\n- Numbers (1, 2, 3...)\n- Custom lists\n- Formulas (auto-adjusts references)\n```\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n- Double-click the fill handle to autofill down to the last adjacent data row\n- Drag right to fill horizontally\n- Use Edit > Paste special for values-only or transpose","codeExamples":[{"id":"ex1","title":"Autofill Series","description":"Practice autofill with days and numbers","code":{"google-sheets":"// Type 'January' in cell A1\n// Drag the fill handle (blue square) down to A12\n// Sheets fills: February, March... December\n\n// Type 10 in B1, 20 in B2\n// Select both, drag down\n// Sheets fills: 30, 40, 50..."},"explanation":"Autofill recognizes patterns in days, months, numbers, and custom sequences."}]}],
