      sections: [{"id":"s1","title":"Named Ranges","whyItMatters":"Named ranges make formulas readable and maintainable. Instead of =SUM(A2:A100), you write =SUM(Revenue), making spreadsheet logic self-documenting.","content":"**Core Concepts:**\n\nA named range assigns a meaningful name to a cell or range of cells. Names are absolute references by default and can be used in any formula across the spreadsheet.\n\n**Creating Named Ranges:**\n- Select range > Data > Named ranges\n- Or use the name box (left of formula bar)\n- Names cannot contain spaces or start with numbers\n- Use underscores or periods: Sales_Data, Q1.Total\n\n**Benefits:**\n\n```text\nWithout Named Range:    With Named Range:\n=VLOOKUP(A2,'Sheet1'!  =VLOOKUP(ProductID,\n  $A$2:$B$100,2,FALSE)  ProductTable,2,FALSE)\n\nMore readable, harder to break\nNamed ranges move when data moves\nDrop-down suggestions as you type\n```\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n- Defined names in Data > Named ranges\n- Names are absolute - they don't change when copied\n- Use names in formulas for self-documenting spreadsheets","codeExamples":[{"id":"ex1","title":"Named Range Demo","description":"Create and use named ranges","code":{"google-sheets":"// Select A1:A10 > Data > Named ranges > 'ProductList'\n// Select B1:B10 > Data > Named ranges > 'Prices'\n// In C1: =SUM(Prices)\n// In D1: =COUNTIF(Prices,\">100\")"},"explanation":"Named ranges like 'Prices' replace hard-coded ranges, making formulas self-documenting."}]}],
