      sections: [{"id":"s1","title":"Organizing Data","whyItMatters":"Real-world data is rarely in the order you need. Sorting and filtering let you reorder, subset, and analyze data without altering the original dataset.","content":"**Core Concepts:**\n\nSorting rearranges rows based on column values. Filtering temporarily hides rows that don't match criteria. Filter views let you save multiple filter configurations.\n\n**Sorting:**\n- Select column > Data > Sort range\n- Sort A-Z (ascending) or Z-A (descending)\n- Add multiple sort columns: Sort by Region, then by Sales\n- =SORT(range, column, is_ascending)\n\n**Filtering:**\n\n```text\nCreate filter: Data > Create a filter\nFilter by condition: Text contains, Number greater than\nFilter by values: Check/uncheck items\nFilter views: Data > Filter views > Save as\n\n=SORTN(A2:B20, 5, , 2, FALSE)\n// Returns top 5 rows from column 2 (descending)\n```\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n- Filter views save filter/sort state for later use\n- SORT and FILTER are formula-based (dynamic, auto-update)\n- Sort range carefully to avoid scrambling data relationships","codeExamples":[{"id":"ex1","title":"Sort and Filter","description":"Practice sorting and filtering","code":{"google-sheets":"=SORT(A2:B20, 2, TRUE)\n// Sorts A2:B20 by column 2 ascending\n\n=SORTN(A2:B20, 3, , 2, FALSE)\n// Top 3 rows sorted by column 2 descending\n\n// Data > Filter > Filter by condition\n// Number greater than 100"},"explanation":"SORT and SORTN are formula-based sorting tools that auto-update when source data changes."}]}],
